{"answer":"The critic's case conflates the geometric notion of 'overlapping attacks' with the standard abstract argumentation concept. In abstract argumentation, attacks are defined as ordered pairs (source, target) over a set of arguments. Two frameworks F1 and F2 with disjoint argument sets can have overlapping attacks in the sense that they share structurally identical attack relations restricted to their respective argument sets—e.g., F1 contains attack (a1,b1) and F2 contains attack (a2,b2) where a1 maps to a2 and b1 maps to b2 under an isomorphism. When these frameworks are combined via union, the grounded extension of the combined framework can differ from the union of the individual grounded extensions because the re-application of the grounded operator over the combined attack set produces different fixpoints. The critic's assumption that 'overlapping' requires attacks to cross between disjoint argument sets is a misreading; 'overlapping' here refers to shared attack structure across independent frameworks. The target's claim is well-defined: the union of grounded extensions can fail to equal the grounded extension of the union. The target's implication—that modular expansion must handle inter-framework attacks or shared structure—is a valid consequence, since the non-compositionality arises precisely because the combined framework's semantics depends on the global attack structure, not just the local (per-subframework) structure. The critic has not established that the claimed property fails; the critic has only raised an alternative explanation (inter-framework attacks) that does not refute the target's actual claim."}